<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hussein Shah of Johor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a>–<a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tengku Hussein, Tengku Long</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hussein Shah was a Malay prince recognised by the British as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> in 1819, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Stamford Raffles</a> needed a ruler to sanction a British post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. Passed over in the succession, he signed the treaty that let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> settle there, and in 1824 ceded the island in return for payments. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1835.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hussein-shah-of-johor.jpg" alt="Hussein Shah of Johor" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Sultan_Hussein_Shah_of_Johor.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
